<h3 style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bottom:6.0pt;margin-lef=
t:
0in;line-height:114%;border:none;mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0p=
t;
mso-border-shadow:yes'><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans";
color:#1B1C1D'>The First Rule - Always know the weight of any load that you=
 are
going to move.</span><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:11.0pt;line-height:=
114%;
color:black'><o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The weight of the lo=
ad
is the primary variable from which all other decisions in a lifting operati=
on
are derived. It dictates the selection of the hoisting equipment, the capac=
ity
requirements of all rigging hardware, and the fundamental configuration of =
the
lift. An unknown or miscalculated load weight is the most direct cause of
overloading, which stands as the most common failure mode in crane-related
accidents.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height=
:114%;
font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>7</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> The determination of
load weight must be approached with methodical precision, using one of three
hierarchical methods.</span><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o:=
p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The most reliable me=
thod
is sourcing <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Direct Information</b>=
. This
involves locating the weight clearly marked by the manufacturer on the load
itself, its crate, or its packaging. If not marked, the weight must be obta=
ined
from official documentation, such as engineered prints, design plans, a bil=
l of
lading, or the manufacturer's catalog specifications.</span><sup><span lang=
=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>9</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o=
:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The second method is=
 <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Direct Measurement</b>. For smaller
components, a calibrated industrial scale provides an exact weight.</span><=
sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>10</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> For larger loads where a scale is impractical, an in-line l=
oad
cell or dynamometer offers a precise solution. This device is mounted betwe=
en
the crane hook and the rigging assembly. During a test lift, where the load=
 is
raised only a few inches from the ground, the dynamometer measures the force
being exerted, providing a direct reading of the total load weight.</span><=
sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>9</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o=
:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The final and least
preferred method is <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Calculation</b=
>, to
be used only when no direct information is available. This is a two-step
process. First, the volume of the load must be determined. For a simple
rectangular object, the volume is its length multiplied by its width,
multiplied by its height. For more complex objects, the rigger must mentally
deconstruct the load into a series of simpler geometric shapes, calculate t=
he
volume of each, and sum them. For a hollow cylinder, such as a large pipe, =
the
volume is calculated using the formula , where<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>is the length,<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>is the wall thickness, and<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>is the outside diameter.</span><su=
p><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>9</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google S=
ans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> Second, this calculated volume must be multiplied by the kn=
own
density of the material. For example, steel has a density of approximately =
490
pounds per cubic foot, while aluminum is 165 pounds per cubic foot, and
concrete is 150 pounds per cubic foot.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>12</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> It is critical to recognize that these are approximations;
factors such as the moisture content of wood or concrete can significantly
increase the actual weight of the load.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>13<o:p></o:p></span></sup></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<h3 style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bottom:6.0pt;margin-lef=
t:
0in;line-height:114%;border:none;mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0p=
t;
mso-border-shadow:yes'><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans";
color:#1B1C1D'>The Second Rule - Always know the center of gravity.</span><=
span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:11.0pt;line-height:114%;color:black'><o:p></o:=
p></span></h3>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The Center of Gravity
(CoG) is the theoretical balance point of an object, the single point at wh=
ich
its entire weight can be considered to be concentrated.</span><sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>14</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> A fundamental law of physics dictates that any freely suspe=
nded
object will orient itself so that its Center of Gravity is positioned direc=
tly
below the point of support&#8212;in this case, the lifting hook.</span><sup=
><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>15</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> This principle is of paramount importance. If a load is rig=
ged
such that the hook is not directly above the true CoG, the load will tilt a=
nd
swing as it is lifted until the CoG finds its stable position. This
uncontrolled movement can lead to the load slipping from its rigging, colli=
ding
with personnel or structures, or becoming dangerously unstable.</span><sup>=
<span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>16</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> Lifting a load from attachment points that are <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>below</i> its Center of Gravity will c=
ause
the load to become top-heavy and invert, a catastrophic failure of the lift=
.</span><sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>18</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
