<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The concept of
&quot;capacity&quot; is more complex than a single number stamped on a mach=
ine.
Personnel must understand the distinction between a machine's rated capacity
and its actual, usable capacity for a specific lift. The <b style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-weight:
normal'>Rated Capacity</b>, also referred to as Gross Capacity, is the maxi=
mum
load the equipment can lift under ideal, manufacturer-specified conditions,=
 as
listed on its load chart or data plate. This value is a theoretical maximum=
 and
is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>not</i> the actual weight that c=
an be
lifted in a real-world scenario.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-s=
ize:
12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-fam=
ily:
"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>2=
0</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>To determine the true
lifting capacity, one must calculate the <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:n=
ormal'>Net
Capacity</b>. This is done by subtracting the weight of all <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Capacity Deductions</b> from the Rated
Capacity. These deductions include the weight of everything attached to or
hanging from the boom tip: the main load block, the headache or overhaul ba=
ll,
all rigging hardware such as slings and shackles, and any stowed jibs or ot=
her
attachments.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-heig=
ht:114%;
font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>20</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> The weight of the l=
oad
itself must never exceed this calculated Net Capacity.</span><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>To visualize this,
consider a typical <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>crane load char=
t</b>.
This chart is a grid. The vertical axis represents the <b style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-weight:
normal'>Load Radius</b>, which is the horizontal distance from the crane's
center of rotation to the load's Center of Gravity. The horizontal axis
represents the <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Boom Length</b>.</s=
pan><sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>22</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> At the intersection of a specific radius and boom length, t=
he
chart provides the Rated Capacity for that configuration. A fundamental
principle of leverage dictates a critical inverse relationship: as the load
radius or the boom length <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>increases=
</i>,
the crane's lifting capacity <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>decrea=
ses</i>
dramatically.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-hei=
ght:
114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Te=
xt";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>21</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> Furthermore, all
footnotes on the chart must be strictly followed, as they specify mandatory
conditions such as outrigger extension and the amount of counterweight requ=
ired
for the listed capacities to be valid.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>20</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>Similarly, a <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>forklift data plate</b> provides the
machine's capacity information. This plate contains the model, serial numbe=
r,
and weight of the forklift.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:1=
2.0pt;
line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Go=
ogle Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>24</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> Critically, it lists
the forklift's capacity at a specific <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:norm=
al'>Load
