lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The WLL is the absol=
ute
maximum load that the equipment is designed to handle safely during normal =
use.
It is a limit that must <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>never</i> be
exceeded.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:=
114%;
font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>50</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> The breaking streng=
th
is not an operational target; it is a failure point used for engineering and
design calculations. The significant difference between the WLL and the
breaking strength is a deliberately engineered margin of safety. This margin
exists to account for a range of real-world variables that are not present =
