<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The center of gravit=
y,
or CoG, is the single, theoretical point within an object where its entire
weight can be considered to be concentrated. It is the point of perfect
balance.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:1=
14%;
font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>12</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> The single most
important objective in any rigging operation&#8212;the Golden Rule of
Lifting&#8212;is to position the lifting hook or attachment point <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>directly above</i> this center of grav=
ity.
If the lifting point is offset from the CoG, the load will inherently seek
equilibrium. Upon being lifted, it will tilt, swing, or slide until its CoG=
 is
vertically aligned underneath the hook. This uncontrolled movement introduc=
es
dangerous dynamic forces and is a primary cause of accidents.</span><sup><s=
pan
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>14</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>Determining the CoG =
is
therefore the rigger's first and most critical calculation. For uniform,
symmetrical objects like a steel cube or a straight pipe, the CoG is locate=
d at
the geometric center.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;
line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Go=
ogle Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>15</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> For complex or
irregular loads, the task is more challenging. One method is the practical
test-lift: the load is rigged based on an estimate of the CoG and lifted on=
ly a
few inches off the ground. The way it tilts reveals the true location of the
CoG relative to the lifting point, allowing the rigger to adjust the rigging
and try again.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-he=
ight:
114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Te=
xt";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>16</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> A more precise meth=
od
involves calculation. For a simple, non-uniform load supported at two point=
s,
the CoG can be found with a straightforward formula. By measuring the weigh=
t at
each end and the distance (or span) between the lifting points, one can cal=
culate
the CoG's location using the relationship: (Weight at Heavier End / Total
Weight) x Span =3D Distance of CoG from the lighter end.</span><sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>17</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> For more complex shapes, the object can be mentally divided
into smaller, regular shapes, the CoG of each is determined, and a weighted
average is calculated to find the CoG of the entire assembly.</span><sup><s=
pan
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>14</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

