mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The basket hitch is a
configuration that supports a load by cradling it from underneath. This
arrangement corresponds directly to the functional description of a
&quot;U-lift.&quot; The term &quot;U-Sling&quot; is standard terminology in=
 the
medical field for patient lifting devices, where a sling forms a U-shape to
support a person.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line=
-height:
114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Te=
xt";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#444746'>30</span></sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'> In industrial riggi=
ng,
the functionally identical configuration is known as a basket hitch. It is
formed by passing the body of the sling under the load, then bringing both =
ends
(eyes or fittings) up for connection to the lifting hook.</span><sup><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>20</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>Visually, the
configuration resembles a basket or hammock supporting the load. Imagine
lifting a large, rectangular wooden crate. A wide synthetic web sling is pa=
ssed
underneath the center of the crate. The two flat eyes at the ends of the sl=
ing
are then brought up and placed together onto the hook of an overhead hoist.=
 The
crate now rests securely in the U-shaped &quot;basket&quot; formed by the
sling.</span><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>This method is
well-suited for lifting loads that are balanced and have a defined shape, s=
uch
as crates, machinery, or welded frames.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>20</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> A key advantage of the basket hitch is that it provides good
control over the load and, unlike a single vertical hitch, prevents the load
from twisting or rotating during the lift.</span><sup><span lang=3DEN
style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>24</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
