mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>The increase in tens=
ion
as the sling angle decreases can be understood through a simple analogy.
Imagine two people carrying a heavy object between them. If they stand close
together, their arms will be nearly vertical (a 90-degree angle to the
horizontal). In this position, they are primarily lifting upwards, and each
person supports half of the object's weight. Now, imagine they move farther
apart. To continue supporting the object, their arms must angle outwards. To
keep the object from falling, they must not only pull upwards to counteract
gravity but also pull inwards, towards each other, to keep their arms from
spreading farther apart. This inward-pulling force adds a significant
horizontal component of tension to their arms. The total tension they feel =
is
the vector sum of the vertical (lifting) force and the horizontal
(inward-pulling) force. The slings in a bridle or basket hitch behave in
precisely the same way.</span><span lang=3DEN style=3D'color:black'><o:p></=
o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:114%;border:=
none;
mso-padding-alt:31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt;mso-border-shadow:yes'><span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-fareast-font-family:"=
Google Sans Text";
mso-bidi-font-family:"Google Sans Text";color:#1B1C1D'>As the horizontal sl=
ing
angle becomes smaller (i.e., the legs spread wider), the horizontal compone=
nt
of the force grows exponentially. This phenomenon is often referred to as a
&quot;force multiplier&quot; because the shallow angle multiplies the tensi=
on
felt by the sling beyond its simple share of the load's weight.</span><sup>=
<span
lang=3DEN style=3D'font-size:12.0pt;line-height:114%;font-family:"Google Sa=
ns Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#444746'>36</span></sup><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Google =
Sans Text";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Google Sans Text";mso-bidi-font-family:"Google San=
s Text";
color:#1B1C1D'> A failure to account for this force multiplication is one of
the most common causes of rigging accidents. A sling that is perfectly adeq=
uate
to lift a load in a vertical configuration can fail catastrophically under =
the
same load if used at too shallow an angle. This demonstrates that the geome=
try
of the lift is as critical to safety as the weight of the load itself. The
rigger is not merely lifting a weight; they are managing the forces within a
geometric system.<o:p></o:p></span></p>

